  <p>Both study hours and attendance show <strong>near-zero and slightly negative correlations</strong> with HSC results (r = -0.022 and r = -0.014, respectively). The highest study-hours quartile (25–29 hrs/week) actually underperforms the lowest quartile (5–11 hrs/week) by 0.053 GPA points (3.990 vs. 4.043).</p>

  <p>These patterns do not imply that studying or attending class is harmful. Rather, they suggest that <strong>quantity of study time and attendance are poor proxies for learning quality</strong>. Students may be spending many hours on ineffective study strategies, or attendance may be high while classroom engagement is low. The data do not capture study quality, teaching effectiveness, or curriculum relevance—factors that likely matter more than raw hours.</p>

  <div class="insight-box warning">
    <strong>Policy Implication: Shift from Time to Quality Metrics</strong>
    If study hours and attendance do not predict outcomes, funding models and school evaluations that reward high attendance or long study schedules may be misaligned with actual learning. Policymakers should explore metrics tied to instructional quality, formative assessment, and student engagement.
  </div>
